# KNOWN GAP — MED ergonomic: a closure whose body contains a D26 fallible-
# arith operator does NOT auto-infer `throws ArithError` on the closure's
# signature.
# ⚡ D45 pin 8 (2026-08-10): the mirror-the-F1a-walker fix shape below is
# SUPERSEDED — D26 auto-infer retires into general inference at round E2, where
# this fixture's INTENDED is re-derived (anonymous-closure decl-site `!` is an
# E2 scout question). Graduates at E2; see decisions.md D45. The F1a pre-`collect_top_level` rewrite pass deliberately
# skips closure bodies (`Expr::Closure`) to avoid
# false-positive promotion of the enclosing fn's signature — the enclosing
# fn's auto-infer walker steps into the closure only if the fallible op is
# in a NON-closure position. Consequence: a closure using `+!` requires the
# user to declare `throws ArithError` on the closure explicitly, which is
# an asymmetry with the fn-body auto-infer.
#
# INTENDED: the closure's body-scan detects `+!` and auto-infers
# `throws ArithError` on the closure's own signature. This fixture then
# compiles + runs, printing `5` (2 + 3 through the closure).
#
# CURRENT: `gg check` REJECTS with `E_UnhandledThrows` at the `+!` inside
# the closure body, because the closure has no explicit `throws` declaration
# and the F1a walker skips closures. The reject is SOUND (not a miscompile,
# not a runtime hazard) — it's an ergonomic gap.
#
# FIX SHAPE: mirror the F1a body-walker at the closure signature-resolution
# site so a closure body containing a fallible-arith op auto-infers
# `throws ArithError` on the closure's signature. Same pattern as the fn-body
# walker in `src/semantic/rewrite.rs::auto_infer_fn`; the differences are the
# storage (closure signature, not `FunctionDef.throws`) and the pass timing
# (closures are resolved lazily during type-check, not at
# `collect_top_level`).
#
# Filed at TODO.md "Semantics / reference-grade rejection > High" as the
# "D26 auto-infer refinement (closure body + smarter capture-vs-propagate
# context detection)" entry. Un-ignore
# `c1_d26_closure_body_no_auto_infer_intended` in `tests/integration.rs`
# when the closure auto-infer lands.
void main():
    Callable[int(int, int)] adder = (int a, int b): a +! b
    int r = adder(2, 3)! catch (e): -1
    print(r)
